# Cloudflare's own rewrite-rules file (same convention Netlify popularized;
# Cloudflare's static-asset serving reads it the same way). Lives in public/
# rather than being written into dist/ by hand for the same reason
# .htaccess does: Expo Router's static export copies everything under
# public/ into dist/ verbatim, so this becomes part of every build
# automatically, including builds Cloudflare Pages runs on its own.
#
# Clean URLs (e.g. /discover -> discover.html) are handled by Cloudflare's
# own default static-asset serving, not listed here.
#
# Dynamic routes: Expo Router prerenders one literal shell file per dynamic
# route pattern (book/[id].html, listing/[id].html), meant to be served for
# ANY id — the 200 status here means "rewrite" (serve this content, keep the
# original URL), not a redirect.
/book/*      /book/[id].html      200
/listing/*   /listing/[id].html   200

# The custom 404 page is handled separately, via wrangler.jsonc's
# assets.not_found_handling ("404-page") — Cloudflare's _redirects here only
# accepts 200/301/302/303/307/308, not 404, so a rewrite line for it would
# either fail validation (as a genuine 404) or silently return 200 for a
# missing page (as a "rewrite"). not_found_handling preserves the real
# status code, which .htaccess's ErrorDocument directive also did.
